he analysis  mario's are going to leave us worse off  so in all scenarios in in pure economic  terms there is a cost what the analysis  shows is that the deal the Prime  Minister has negotiated is by far the  best outcome for the economy and that  while getting the political benefits of  being out of the European Union the  economic costs of that are very minimal  in the deal that the Prime Minister has  negotiated it's interesting though  because you do have to wonder did people  vote to be poorer when they look at this  analysis what are they going to think  because for the UK economy to be buoyant  and sustained what it's doing now it  should stay in the EU is that what this  report is going to show us no the UK  economy is buoyant and and fundamentally  strong and we'd just be better off  staying inside the European well let me  be clear the UK economy will grow  outside the European Union and it will  continue to grow because of its  fundamental strengths it's very strong  high-tech industry our very important  services sector our global companies all  of those things will keep the UK economy  growing but the economy will be in in in  any of these scenarios a little bit  smaller than it would have been if we'd  remained inside the European Union I'm  the Chancellor of course I have to look  at the economic impacts but I readily  accept that it is not only the economy  that influences people they will be  looking at other aspects of the package  the benefits of being able to operate an  independent trade policy of having  control of our fishing waters for  example so there are other aspects as  well the important thing about this  report today is that it informs  Parliament it provides information so  that my colleagues in Parliament when  they're making their decisions about how  to vote on December the 11th can be  properly and fully informed well you are  the Chancellor you endorsing this  analysis coming out this afternoon was  your assessment of the impact 