 of its structures  those who are concerned about our trade  and our economic relationship with  Europe will be assured that we will  continue to have a very close trading an  economic and security relationship with  Europe and I hope it will be the  catalyst to start bringing the country  back together again the Prime Minister  has been very clear we are leaving the  EU but what isn't clear is whether this  deal will make it through pom and the  big question people want the answer to  is  what happens if this deal doesn't make  it through what next well look the Prime  Minister's deal has plenty of critics  but what I haven't heard yet is anybody  with a credible alternative plan this is  the best negotiated deal that is  available from the European Union and if  it is not passed by Parliament we will  be in quite literally unknown territory  we'll be going back to square one and  Parliament politicians will have to sit  down and decide where we go next but our  focus for the next two weeks will be on  selling this deal to the British people  explaining the benefits of it explaining  how to the British people you should be  selling it to the MPs you need to vote  for it you're selling it to the people  that would imply that you're holding  your hands up and saying at some point  there is going to be a second referendum  and we need to get voters onside no not  at all I think that is to misunderstand  how parliamentarians work and operate  parliamentarians are elected to  represent the their constituents and  they do listen whatever people might  think parliamentarians do listen to what  their constituents are saying to them  where public opinion is going and of  course we'll be talking to our  parliamentary colleagues but we also  need to talk to the people of this  country and explain why this is the best  way forward to deliver on the commitment  to leave the European Union but to do it  in a way that will protect British jobs  British businesses and British  prosperity going forward and I am  absolutely convinced that the Prime  Minister's deal is the best way to take  the British economy forward while  leaving the European Union prime  minister is a busy woman at the moment  she's trying to convince the public  she's trying to convince MP she's off to  a g20 summit  she's also promised to debate the brexit  deal with Jeremy Corbyn has she set a  date for that when will she do it well  we've said we've set out the challenge  to Jeremy Corbyn the Prime Minister has  challenged him to a debate it's for him  to respond now and then of course for  the broadcasters to make the detailed  arrangements how about the 9th I'm  looking at my diary that's the date  that's been suggested I'm freeing you  well that suits you does it ok I'll let  the Prime Minister know that's a good  one for you  okay that's pretty an in pencil for now  just in pencil yeah reports today  suggesting that the Prime Minister's  going to refuse to publish in for the  legal advice behind her deal well she  published that legal advice because  Jacob Riis Morgan's come out today and  said look she promised to do that it was  requested by Humble address and to not  publish it in full would be  unconstitutional on the contrary it's  the other way around we will publish a  summary of the legal advice but the  clear constitutional in for why summary  well I'll just explain that now the  clear constitutional precedent in this  country is that the advice of the  Attorney General to the government is  private and that has to be the case if  government is going to be able to  function properly the government has to  have access to private legal advice so  that it can make decisions which are  properly made and indeed so that it can  ensure that it complies with its legal  obligations and if we started publishing  the advice of the Attorney General to  government we would find that government  could not operate freely and could not  obtain the legal advice that it needs in  order to navigate ordinary processes of  government a couple of last questions  for you cuz I know you've got busy day  ahead and the penultimate one what  preparations are happening for no deal  because there's a report this morning a  parliamentary report that has said the  secrecy and shortcomings in the  preparations for a no deal by the  government are potentially toxic  combination we're running a report today  showing that port authorities say the  Department of Transport are leaving it  all a bit late and it could cause chaos  well look there's a balance to be struck  isn't that we've got to prepare for the  contingency of an Odile exit there is a  possibility that that could happen so  it's right that we're planning it's  right that we're spending money but  equally we don't think that this is a  likely outcome even though we have to  prepare for it as a possibility and  people would rightly ask after the event  whether we've been cautious in the way  we've spent public money in preparing  for something that more than likely will  never happen so there is a balancing act  I think we've got it about right  we don't want to panic people  unnecessarily we don't want to spend  taxpayers money unnecessarily but we do  want to do the things that are vitally  important to have been done if we were  to find ourselves in a no deal scenario  okay well we've got you as the nation's  money man I'm looking for a definitive  answer from you this morning on  something that's really been dividing  sky news viewers this morning and that  is who pays for Christmas dinner are you  hosting this year and would you ask  relatives for a contribution because  let's face it if you hosting up to 10  people it could be pretty pricey well no  I wouldn't ask relatives for a financial  contribution but my experience is that  when you host a lunch party or a dinner  party very often people bring you know  bottle of wine bottle of whiskey or  something with them so people people  often will bring you know contributions  in kind I think that's a nicer way to do  it than asking people for cash well Mr  Hammond you might want to change your  mind this year because I hear food  prices are going up if there's a No Deal  saturate well if there's no deal it's  quite likely that there would be a  negative impact on food prices we import  a lot of food including a lot of food  from the European Union in a no deal  scenario we could expect the cost of  that to increase YUM  okay well what a No Deal and the deal  isn't around yet we can spend a bit of  cash on our loved ones and host them for  Christmas dinner that's the definitive  answer from the money man of the nation  mr. Philip Hammond of the Chancellor  thank you very much thank you Philip  Hammond thank you

